call_details.response:
The board has 10 empty positions remaining, with both players having 16 pieces and player x to move. The only legal columns are 0, 1, 2, 5 and 6. Exhaustive enumeration of every 4-in-a-row line on the board shows that the sole remaining winning patterns are:

- player o completes a horizontal four by occupying (3,0),
- player o completes a diagonal (or vertical) four by occupying (0,6),
- player x completes a vertical four inside column 0 by occupying all four cells of either window (rows 1-4) or (rows 0-3).

Gravity implies that (3,0) becomes available only after column 0 has been played once, and (0,6) becomes available only after column 6 has been played once. Consequently any move that occupies the cell immediately below either of those two squares arms an immediate winning threat for o. Because it would then be o’s turn, o would simply take the armed square and win. Columns 0 and 6 are therefore instant losses for x.

The three remaining moves (columns 1, 2 and 5) place a piece on the top row of a column that participates in none of the surviving winning lines; they are pure tempo moves. After any of those moves the game reduces to an elementary end-game on two “pair” columns (the two-cell stacks that finish at o’s winning squares) together with three neutral tempo squares and the five-cell stack of column 0. Optimal play proceeds as follows:

- x never arms either pair (doing so would lose at once);
- whenever o arms a pair, x immediately blocks the newly created threat;
- the three neutral squares are exhausted;
- the parity of the remaining cells forces o to be the player who first opens column 0; x blocks at (3,0);
- the three cells above (3,0) then form a single vertical threat stack for x. Because the threat is only single, o can always answer the final square of that stack, preventing x from ever obtaining four consecutive cells.

The identical drawing line is reached no matter which of the three tempo columns x chooses first. Hence columns 1, 2 and 5 are equivalent and optimal.

Final Answer: 2

